Accept incoming Bluetooth connections as the peripheral.

A computer paired to the speaker could not use it as a microphone: Windows
built the hands-free endpoints, but opening the microphone failed every time
the computer had connected in. A Windows HCI trace showed why - the computer's
Setup Synchronous Connection (eSCO) came back from this controller with
Limited Resources (0x0D). The MT8163's Bluetooth firmware refuses the remote's
eSCO setup whenever it is the central of the ACL link.

It is the central on every incoming link because the kernel accepts with role
0x00 ("become central") when a listener asks for HCI_LM_MASTER, and BlueZ's
A2DP (AVDTP) listener does. Windows then refuses the switch back (0x21), so a
userspace fix after the connection completes cannot recover the link.

Measured on Device 1 with a Windows 11 laptop:

  Speaker peripheral (bluetoothd without A2DP)   3/3 eSCO opens succeed
  Speaker central (stock accept)                 eSCO refused 0x0D
  Switch Role to peripheral after connecting     refused 0x21

So accept every incoming ACL as the peripheral. Outgoing connections are left
alone: those already offer the remote a role switch, and a remote that takes
it leaves this side the peripheral.

The cost is scatternet. With two sources connected at once, the speaker is now
a peripheral in two piconets rather than the central of one, which the
controller must time-share. BLE (the Home Assistant Bluetooth proxy) is a
different link type and does not pass through here.

--- a/net/bluetooth/hci_event.c
+++ b/net/bluetooth/hci_event.c
@@ -3340,10 +3340,15 @@
 
 		bacpy(&cp.bdaddr, &ev->bdaddr);
 
-		if (lmp_rswitch_capable(hdev) && (mask & HCI_LM_MASTER))
-			cp.role = 0x00; /* Become central */
-		else
-			cp.role = 0x01; /* Remain peripheral */
+		/* Always remain peripheral on this device. The MT8163's
+		 * Bluetooth firmware refuses a remote's eSCO setup with
+		 * Limited Resources (0x0D) whenever it is the central of the
+		 * ACL, so a computer connecting in could never open its
+		 * hands-free audio. The request to become central comes from
+		 * BlueZ's A2DP listener (HCI_LM_MASTER), and remotes such as
+		 * Windows then refuse a later switch back (0x21).
+		 */
+		cp.role = 0x01; /* Remain peripheral */
 
 		hci_send_cmd(hdev, HCI_OP_ACCEPT_CONN_REQ, sizeof(cp), &cp);
 	} else if (!(flags & HCI_PROTO_DEFER)) {
